{"status":"answered","answer":"Under the Loan Late Payment and Collections Policy v1.6, your consumer loan payment is treated as late only if it isn't received within 15 calendar days after the due date on your billing statement. Payments made within that grace period count as on-time for fee purposes (though interest keeps accruing daily on simple-interest loans until payment arrives). If the last day of the grace period falls on a weekend or Federal Reserve holiday, it extends to the next business day.

The late fee is 5% of the scheduled payment amount or $25.00, whichever is less. Only one late fee is charged per scheduled payment, and you're never charged a new late fee just because an earlier one is unpaid. No late fees apply while you're meeting the terms of an approved hardship program.

This covers closed-end consumer loans (new/used auto, personal and share-secured loans); credit cards, HELOCs and first mortgages have their own agreements.","cited_chunk_ids":["loan_late_payment_policy::layout::B_structure::0002","loan_late_payment_policy::layout::B_structure::0003","loan_late_payment_policy::layout::B_structure::0001"],"reason":"The passages directly state the 15-day grace period and the late fee amount and conditions for consumer loans."}
